  faqs={[
    {q:"Which neighborhood is best for expats in Panama City?", a:"It depends on your lifestyle. Punta Pacífica and Punta Paitilla offer the highest security and the largest expat community, ideal for those who prioritize convenience and ocean views. Costa del Este suits families with children in international schools. El Cangrejo and Bella Vista are the best value for singles and couples who want walkability and nightlife. San Francisco is a solid middle ground."},
    {q:"Is Panama City safe for expats?", a:"Expat-focused neighborhoods — Punta Pacífica, Punta Paitilla, Costa del Este, San Francisco — are generally safe with normal urban precautions. Panama City has significant inequality, and areas like El Chorrillo or parts of the old city can be unsafe at night. Living in vetted residential buildings and using reputable taxis or ridesharing apps (InDriver, Uber) eliminates most risk for day-to-day life."},
    {q:"What does it cost to live in Panama City per month?", a:"Panama City is Panama's most expensive area. A single professional can manage on $2,000–$2,800/month; a couple in a mid-range apartment typically spends $3,000–$4,500/month including rent ($1,200–$2,000), groceries, utilities, transport, and dining out. Living in a luxury high-rise or frequently using premium services easily pushes that to $5,000+."},
    {q:"How is the healthcare system in Panama City?", a:"Panama City has world-class private hospitals. Hospital Punta Pacífica is a Johns Hopkins International affiliate — one of the top facilities in Latin America. Pacífica Salud, Centro Médico Paitilla, and Hospital Nacional offer excellent specialist care. Private health insurance is strongly recommended; costs are typically 40–60% lower than equivalent care in the US, and quality for elective and specialist treatment is comparable."},
    {q:"What visa options are best for working professionals and investors?", a:"The Friendly Nations visa is the most popular for professionals from eligible countries — it requires demonstrating economic ties (a job offer, business ownership, or a bank deposit) and grants permanent residency. The Qualified Investor visa (minimum $300,000 investment in real estate or approved assets) offers fast-track residency. Remote workers can use the Short Stay Permit, which allows 9 months per year without establishing full residency."},
